    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only executable test class, and both its cases pass along with a `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec asked that the owner response include `membershipTier`: `SILVER` when `namesakeCount` is 0 and an email is present, otherwise `BRONZE`.\n\nThe endpoint responds by mapping the `Owner` entity to an `OwnerDto` (generated from `openapi.yml`) via `OwnerMapper`, which auto-maps derived read-only properties by name \u2014 the same mechanism already used for `initials` (a `getInitials()` getter on `Person`). I followed that established pattern rather than adding a new pipeline step or duplicating logic:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `membershipTier` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field (and it's documented in the API contract).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a derived `getMembershipTier()` getter next to `namesakeCount`/`email`, returning `SILVER`/`BRONZE` per the rule. It reuses `StringUtils.hasText` for the \"email present\" check (consistent with `NormalizeOwnerEmail`) and treats a null `namesakeCount` as not-unique \u2192 `BRONZE`.\n\nMapStruct auto-maps the new getter to the DTO field, so no mapper or function changes were needed. The logic lives on the entity as a single-responsibility derived accessor, mirroring the existing `initials` derivation.",
